.\" getusershell.3 - the permitted login shells
.TH GETUSERSHELL 3 "2026-10-05" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
getusershell, setusershell, endusershell \- get the permitted login shells
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B "char *getusershell(void);"
.br
.B "void setusershell(void);"
.br
.B "void endusershell(void);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR getusershell ()
function returns the next shell from the list of permitted login shells in
.IR /etc/shells ,
opening the file on the first call.
.PP
Each line of
.I /etc/shells
names one shell by its absolute pathname.
Leading blanks are skipped, the name ends at a blank or a
.RB ' # ',
and a line that does not then begin with
.RB ' / '
is ignored, so that blank lines and comments may be used freely.
.PP
If
.I /etc/shells
does not exist, the list is
.I /bin/sh
alone.
.PP
The
.BR setusershell ()
function rewinds the list, so that the next call to
.BR getusershell ()
returns its first entry.
The
.BR endusershell ()
function closes the file.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The
.BR getusershell ()
function returns a pointer to the pathname, in a static buffer that the
next call overwrites, or NULL at the end of the list.
It does not set
.IR errno .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/shells
The permitted login shells, one per line.
.SH NOTES
The functions share one static buffer and one open file, and are not
thread-safe.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following program reports whether its argument is a permitted shell.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int
main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    char *shell;
    int found = 0;

    if (argc != 2)
        return 2;
    setusershell();
    while ((shell = getusershell()) != NULL) {
        if (strcmp(shell, argv[1]) == 0) {
            found = 1;
            break;
        }
    }
    endusershell();
    printf("%s is %sa permitted shell\en", argv[1], found ? "" : "not ");
    return !found;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR getpwent (3),
.BR crypt (3)
.SH STANDARDS
4.3BSD.
Not in POSIX.
